 "constraints": [],
 "stem": "Use the general rule to solve $x^2 + {=b}x - {=-c} = 0$. Give the positive solution, rounded to the nearest hundredth.",
 "answer": "fixed(x1, 2)",
 "solution": [
  "Here $a=1$, $b={=b}$, $c={=c}$.",
  "$x=\\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}=\\frac{-{=b}\\pm\\sqrt{{=D}}}{2}$, and $\\sqrt{{=D}}\\approx{=fixed(s,3)}$.",
  "$x=\\frac{-{=b}+{=fixed(s,3)}}{2}\\approx{=fixed(x1,2)}$ or $x=\\frac{-{=b}-{=fixed(s,3)}}{2}\\approx{=fixed(x2,2)}$.",
  "The positive solution is ${=fixed(x1,2)}$."
 ],
 "proposed_misconceptions": [],
 "notes": "c is negative so the equation is written with minus sign; roots always opposite sign, exactly one positive."
}
